# Goal + recognised scene state -> subtask sequence.
#
# A separate node, and a service rather than a library call, because the
# planner is the piece most likely to be replaced. Today it is a lookup table
# read off the demonstrations (216/217 of the operators' own sequences); the
# intent is to swap it for a VLM without touching the manager. Keeping it
# behind this request is what makes that a node swap instead of a rewrite.
#
# Called once per episode start and once per replan, so a blocking round trip
# is fine here -- nothing in the 30 Hz path waits on it.

# kit | pick | place.
string goal

# Fused object state: target | flipped | in_gripper | unknown_pose.
#
# Already fused by the caller, because whether the part is held is read from
# the gripper rather than from vision -- the dataset has too few failed
# grasps to fit a classifier, while width and effort separate "closed on
# something" from "closed on air" by construction. That is a fact about the
# cell's sensors, so it stays with the manager; this service answers only
# what to do about a state once it is known.
string state
---
# False when the state has no plan. That is not a retry condition: an object
# in neither canonical pose, or a probe that abstained, needs an operator.
# Guessing there is how a cell damages a part.
bool success

# Why there is no plan. Populated only on failure.
string reason

# Neutral goal sentence, which the policy prompt's "Task:" field gets.
# Neutral because the recorded sentences name the method ("flip it, then pick
# it up"), which would hand the policy the answer it is supposed to read off
# the image.
string task_prompt

# Subtask short names, in order, starting with the one to run now. Empty with
# success=true means the goal is already satisfied.
string[] subtasks
